%[text] ## Problem 1
%[text] Consider the BVP on $\[0, 1\]$:
%[text] $&dollar&;&dollar&;\n-\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\left( \\left(1 + x^2\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d} u}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\right) = 2, \\quad 0 \< x \< 1,\\quad u(0) = 0,\\quad u'(1) = -3.\n&dollar&;&dollar&;$
%[text] Use **one linear element** on $\[0, 1\]$, with nodes $x\_{1} = 0$ and $x\_{2} = 1$.
%[text] Let $\\psi\_{i}$ be the linear shape functions and write $u\_{h}(x) = U\_{1}\\psi\_{1}(x) + U\_{2}\\psi\_{2}(x)$.
%[text] **Matlab is nor permitted for this exercise.** Select one answer per part. Numerical choices are rounded to five decimal places. An incorrect choice subtracts 25% of the points for the part. Select "Empty answer (no penalty)" to receive zero points without a penalty,
%[text] (a) (2 points) Compute $K\_{11}$, the $(1,1)$ entry of the stiffness matrix $K$, before imposing boundary conditions.
%[text] - `1.33333 ✔️`
%[text] - `1.00000`
%[text] - `2.66667`
%[text] - `0.66667`
%[text] - Empty answer (no penalty) \
%[text] **Solution**
%[text] The shape functions of the element and their derivatives are,
%[text] $\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\psi\_{1} (x) = \\frac{x - 1}{0-1} = 1-x, &\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{1}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x) = -1,\\\\\n\\psi\_{2}(x) = \\frac{x-0}{1-0} = x, & \n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{2}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x) = 1.\n\\end{array}$
%[text] So the components of the stiffness matrix are,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rcl}\nK\_{ij}\\!\\!\\! &=&\\!\\!\\! \\int\_{x\_{1}}^{x\_{2}} a\_{1}(x) \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{i}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{j}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\,\\mathrm{d} x =\n\\int\_{0}^{1} \\left(1 + x^{2}\\right) \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{i}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\psi\_{j}}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\,\\mathrm{d} x \\\\\n \\!\\!\\! &=&\\!\\!\\! (-1)^{i + j} \\int\_{0}^{1} \\left(1 + x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x = (-1)^{i+ j}\n\\left\[ x + \\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right\]\_{x = 0}^{x = 1} = \\frac{4}{3} (-1)^{i+j},\\quad i, j = 1,2.\n\\end{array}$
%[text] and them, the stiffness matrix is,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $K = \\left(\\!\\begin{array}{rr}\n4 / 3 & -4/3\\\\\n-4/3 & 4/3\n\\end{array}\\!\\right),$
%[text] so $K\_{11} = 4 / 3 \\approx 1.33333$. 
%[text] We can check this result using Matlab:
clearvars
close all
syms x;
nodes = [0; 1];
h = nodes(2) - nodes(1);
a(x) = 1+x^2;
f = 2;
U0 = 0;
DU1 = -3;

Psi(x) = [nodes(2)-x, x-nodes(1)] / h;

K = int(1+x.^2,x,nodes(1),nodes(2)) * [1, -1; -1, 1] / h^2 %[output:9196b165]
fprintf("K(1,1) = %.5f\n", K(1,1)) %[output:54fa55c3]
%[text] (b) (2 points) Compute the source load $F\_{1}$.
%[text] - Empty answer (no penalty)
%[text] - `2.00000`
%[text] - `-1.00000`
%[text] - `1.00000 ✔️`
%[text] - `0.50000` \
%[text] **Solution**
%[text] According to the derived formulas, the components the local source load vector, $F$, of the element are given by,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{r}\nF\_{i} = \\int\_{x\_{1}}^{x\_{2}} f(x)\\psi\_{i} (x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\quad i = 1, 2.\n\\end{array}$
%[text] So,  for this case in point,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{l}\nF\_{1} = \\int\_{0}^{1} 2\\left(1 - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = 2\\left\[ x - \\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right\]\_{x = 0}^{x = 1} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 1,\\\\\nF\_{2} = \\int\_{0}^{1} 2 x\\,\\mathrm{d} x = 2\\left\[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right\]\_{x = 0}^{x = 1} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 1.\n\\end{array}$
%[text] Hence $F = \\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c} 1\\\\ 1\\end{array}\\!\\right)$, and $F\_{1} = 1$. 
%[text] **Remark:** we note that in this case, as the source function is constant, $f(x)\\equiv 2$, we can apply straight the formulas in the cheat-sheet, and save the calculus of the integrals. 
%[text] We can check this result using Matlab:
F = int(f * Psi(x)',x,nodes(1),nodes(2)) %[output:8df8659c]
fprintf("F(1) = %.5f", F(1)) %[output:0a18abed]
%[text] (c) (4 points) Compute the FEM approximation using the given boundary conditions. Give the average of this nodal values, $\\bar{U} = \\left( U\_{1} + U\_{2} \\right) / 2$.
%[text] - `-3.75000`
%[text] - `-0.93750`
%[text] - Empty answer (no penalty)
%[text] - `-1.87500 ✔️`
%[text] - `1.87500` \
%[text] **Solution**
%[text] Using the previous results, we see that the system for the nodal values $U\_{1}$ and $U\_{2}$ turns out to be
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{lr}\n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{rr}\n4 / 3 & -4 / 3\\\\\n-4 / 3 & 4 / 3\n\\end{array}\\!\\right)\n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c}\nU\_{1}\\\\\nU\_{2}\n\\end{array}\\!\\right) = \n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c}\n1\\\\\n1\n\\end{array}\\!\\right) +\n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c}\nQ\_{1}\\\\\nQ\_{2}\n\\end{array}\\!\\right).\n& (\\ast)\n\\end{array} $
%[text] Whereas the required values for $u$ and $u'$ at $x = 0$ and $x=1$ respectively, recall:  $u(0) = 0$, and $u'(1) = -3$, lead to the following boundary conditions for the FEM formulation,   
%[text] - Natural B.C.: $Q\_{2} = \\left. a\_{1}(x)\\frac{\\mathrm{d} u}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\right|\_{x = x\_{2}} = \\left.\\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right) \\frac{\\mathrm{d} u}{\\mathrm{d} x}(x)\\right|\_{x = 1} = 2 u'(1) = 2\\times (-3) = -6$.
%[text] - Essential B.C.: $U\_{1} = u(0) = 0$. \
%[text] So the reduced system is made just for the second equation of the system $(\\ast)$, setting $Q\_{2} = -6$ and $U\_{1} = 0$. Hence,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{4}{3} U\_{2} = -5$.
%[text] Therefore, our FEA (Finite Element Analysis) of the BVP posed at the problem's statement yields the nodal solution: $U\_{1} = 0$ (straight from the essential B.C.) and $U\_{2} = -15 / 4 = 3.75$.
%[text] **Post process:** (**not** asked in the exercise) now we can get the secondary variables (the $Q$'s) that are not specified by the natural boundary conditions ($Q\_{1}$ in our case) substituting in $(\\ast)$ the values of the primary variables (the $U$'s)  already found (the values of $U\_{1}$ and $U\_{2}$ in our case). Indeed: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c}\nQ\_{1}\\\\\nQ\_{2}\n\\end{array}\\!\\right)\n= \n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{rr}\n4 / 3 & - 4 / 3\\\\\n-4 / 3 &  4 / 3\n\\end{array}\\!\\right)\n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{l}\nU\_{1} = 0\\\\\nU\_{2} = -15 / 4\n\\end{array}\\!\\right) -\n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{c}\n1\\\\\n1\n\\end{array}\\!\\right) = \n\\left(\\!\\begin{array}{r}\n4\\\\\n-6\n \\end{array}\\!\\right)$.
%[text] and hence, $Q\_{1} = 4$. **Note:** the post process is not
%[text] Finally, the nodal value tolls: $\\bar{U} = \\frac{ U\_{1} + U\_{2} }{ 2 } = \\frac{0 - \\frac{15}{4}}{2} = -\\frac{15}{8} = -1.875$.
%[text] We can check this result using Matlab. **Note:** recall the matrix $K$ and the vector $F$ were already computed in items $(a)$ and $(b)$respectively, so we need to allocate memory only for vectors $u$ and $Q$. 
Q = zeros(2,1);
u = zeros(2,1);

fixedNods = 1;
freeNods = 2;

%Boundary Conditions (B.C.):
% Natural
Q(2) = a(nodes(2))*DU1;
% Essential
u(1) = 0;

%Reduced system
Fm = Q(freeNods) + F(freeNods) - K(freeNods,fixedNods)*u(fixedNods) %[output:9dcc65fc]
Km = K(freeNods,freeNods);
um = Km \ Fm;
u(freeNods) = um;
fprintf("u(1) = %.5f\nu(2) = %.5f\n", u(1), u(2)); %[output:561ee8a8]
%[text] Compute the average:
uBar = (u(1) + u(2)) / 2;
fprintf("Averaged U = %.5f", uBar) %[output:78b4c6be]
%[text] (d) (2 points) The exact solution is $u(x) = - 4 \\arctan(x) - \\ln\\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right)$. At which point $\[0, 1\]$ does the absolute error $\\left| u(x) - u\_{h}(x) \\right|$ attain its maximum? Select the coordinate $x$
%[text] - `1.00000`
%[text] - Empty answer (no penalty)
%[text] - `0.13789`
%[text] - `0.00000`
%[text] - `0.63785 ✔️` \
%[text] **Hint:** The maximum absolute error is attained at an interior point where $u - u\_{h}$ has a local minimum. Recall also that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\arctan (x) = \\frac{ 1 }{ 1 + x^{2} }$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} x} \\ln\\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right) = \\frac{ 2 x }{ 1 + x^{2} }$.
%[text] **Solution**
%[text] Let us denote $\\mathcal{E}(x) = \\left| u(x) - u\_{h}(x)\\right|$. It is clear that $\\mathcal{E}(x)$ is a continuous function at the bounded and closed interval $I = \[0, 1\]$, since it is the absolute value of the sum of elementary continuous functions. Therefore according to the *Weierstrass theorem in absolute extrema*, $\\mathcal{E}(x)$ attains an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum at least once in $I$. In addition, we define, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $&dollar&;&dollar&;\nw(x) = u(x) - u\_{h}(x) = \\frac{15}{4} x - 4\\arctan (x) - \\ln\\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right)\n&dollar&;&dollar&;$. 
%[text] $w(x)$ is differentiable for $0 \< x \< 1$ with derivative,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $w'(x) = \\frac{15}{4} - \\frac{ 4 }{ 1 + x^{2} } - \\frac{ 2 x }{ 1 + x^{2} }\n = \\frac{ 15 x^{2} - 8 x - 1}{4 \\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right) } \n = \\frac{\\left( x - \\frac{4 - \\sqrt{31}}{15}\\right)\n\\left( x - \\frac{4 + \\sqrt{31}}{15}\\right)}{4\\left(1 + x^{2}\\right)}$,
%[text] Then we see that, at the interval $I = \[0,1\]$: 
%[text] - $w(0) = 0$,
%[text] - $w(x)$ decreases at the interval $I\_{1} = \\left(0, \\frac{4 + \\sqrt{31}}{15}\\right)$, since $w'(x) \< 0$ for $0 \< x \< \\frac{4 + \\sqrt{31}}{15}\\approx 0.63785$,
%[text] - $w(x)$ increases at the interval $I\_{2} =\\left(\\frac{ 4 + \\sqrt{31} }{4} ,1\\right)$, since $w'(x) \> 0$ for $\\frac{ 4 + \\sqrt{31} }{4} \< x \< 1$  and,
%[text] - $w(1) = \\frac{15}{4} - \\pi - \\ln 2\\approx -0.08474 \< 0$,  \
%[text] so we conclude  that $w(x) \\le 0$ at $I$, and then $\\mathcal{E} (x)= -w(x)$ for $x\\in I$ (see Figure 1 below). This implies that $\\mathcal{E}(x)$ is differentiable for all the points at the interior of $I$, $\\overset{\\circ}{I}=(0,1)$, with $\\mathcal{E}'(x) = -w'(x)$. Hence, the absolute maximum and minimum values of $\\mathcal{E}(x)$ at $I$ are reached either at the critical points (i.e., at the zeros of its derivative) in $\\overset{\\circ}{I}$, or at the extrema of the interval (points $x = 0$, and $x = 1$). For $\\mathcal{E}(x)$, the derivative at $\\overset{\\circ}{I}$ vanishes only at the point,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $x = \\frac{4 + \\sqrt{31}}{15}$ 
%[text] (see the above discussion on the changes of sign of $w'(x)$ at the interval $I$) and, as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathcal{E}(0) = 0 \< \\mathcal{E}(1) = \\pi + \\ln 2 - \\frac{15}{4}\\approx 0.08474 \< \\mathcal{E}\\left(\\frac{4 + \\sqrt{31}}{15}\\right)\\approx 0.22056$,
%[text] then, clearly, $\\arg\\max\\limits\_{0\\le x \\le 1}\\mathcal{E}(x) = \\arg\\max\\limits\_{0\\le x \\le 1} \\left| u(x) - u\_{h}(x)\\right| = \\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{15} + \\frac{4}{15} \\approx 0.63785$ (see Figure 1 below). 
%[text] **Note:** of course, to answer this part, it was enough to follow the **Hint** and find the interior point where $u - u\_{h}$ has a local minimum, without any prior discussion.
%[text] We can check this result using Matlab:
err(x) = - 4 * atan(x) - log(1+x.^2) - Psi(x) * u;
pretty(err(x)) %[output:99ca8d40]
derr = diff(err(x),x) %[output:4fd83184]
%[text] Compute the critical points of $w(x) = u(x) - u\_{h}(x) =  \\frac{15}{4} x - 4\\arctan(x) - \\ln\\left( 1 + x^{2} \\right)$
eqn = simplify(derr);
pretty(eqn) %[output:105e5335]
sols = solve(4 * (1 + x^2) * eqn,x);
pretty(sols) %[output:40635a99]
solsFP = double(sols);
maxPoint = [solsFP(2), abs(double(err(solsFP(2))))];
t = nodes(1):0.01:nodes(2);
errValues = double(err(t));
shift = 0.01;
plot(t,abs(errValues),'-') %[output:2a5d08ab]
hold on %[output:2a5d08ab]
plot(maxPoint(1),maxPoint(2),'o',MarkerFaceColor='auto') %[output:2a5d08ab]
xlabel('$x$',Interpreter='LaTeX',FontSize=12) %[output:2a5d08ab]
ylabel('$\mathcal{E}(x)$',Interpreter='LaTeX',FontSize=12, Rotation=0) %[output:2a5d08ab]
title("Figure 1. $\mathcal{E}(x) = \left| u(x) - u_{h}(x)\right|$",Interpreter="latex", FontSize=12) %[output:2a5d08ab]
text(maxPoint(1)+shift,maxPoint(2)+shift, ... %[output:2a5d08ab]
    "$("+num2str(maxPoint(1))+", "+num2str(maxPoint(2))+")$",Interpreter="latex",... %[output:2a5d08ab]
    FontSize=10) %[output:2a5d08ab]
hold off %[output:2a5d08ab]
fprintf("El valor màxim del error a l'interval [%d,%d] s'assoleix al punt x %c %.5f", ... %[output:group:4089b99e] %[output:6a63bc8d]
    nodes(1),nodes(2), char(0x2248), double(sols(2))) %[output:group:4089b99e] %[output:6a63bc8d]

